<div class="wrap page-head"><p class="kicker">Part of the Orientale Lumen Foundation</p><h1>Society of St. John Chrysostom</h1><p class="lede">The lay society, founded in England in 1926, whose United States chapter organized the first Orientale Lumen conference and gave the Foundation its origins.</p></div>
<section class="band" style="padding-top:1.5rem">
  <div class="wrap two-col top">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="dropcap">Saint John Chrysostom was born in Antioch around 349 and was Archbishop of Constantinople from about 397 to 407, a period of reform and renewal among the clergy and faithful of the Byzantine Empire. Twice exiled by his enemies and the imperial court, he died at Comana in Pontus. For his preaching he is called Chrysostom, Golden Mouth, and the Divine Liturgy that bears his name is the most celebrated in the Byzantine Churches.</p>
      <h2>What the Society does</h2>
      <p>Makes known the history, worship, spirituality, discipline, and theology of Eastern Christendom. Works and prays that all Christians, particularly the Orthodox and Catholic Churches, will attain the fullness of unity Jesus Christ desires. Develops educational programs about the Eastern Churches, organizes pilgrimages and conferences that encourage ecumenical dialogue, supports Eastern Christian communities in need, publishes on Eastern Christianity, encourages the study of Saint John Chrysostom, and promotes scholarships for the study of the Eastern Churches in Catholic and Orthodox seminaries.</p>
      <h2>Chapters</h2>
      <p>The national chapter for the United States is in Washington, DC, and organizes the annual Orientale Lumen Conference. Local chapters meet in several other cities, and new ones can be started by any group interested in the Society's goals. <a class="textlink" href="/contact/">Contact the national office →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <img src="/images/holy-fathers.jpg" alt="Icon of the Holy Fathers" loading="lazy" style="max-width:380px">
